# Travel (doc 62, phase 7): journeys made and wanted, the roads and what's on them (bandits, mud, inns, tolls), carters
# and caravans, the other towns as folk here see them, the sea out of Ridgemere, pilgrims, those who never left, the
# travellers who pass through, and the pull of where you grew up. Region-only scenes: regions/land7_regions.talk.
# Alternatives run in parallel. A tagged reply sets a mark (#x) so the close that follows it can answer it (a@x).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Journeys wanted

scene e5_tr_one_day
when topic=travel
a : One day I'm going to see the whole realm. Every town. | I've a mind to walk the Southway end to end, just to say I have. | If I had a month and a mule, I'd be gone tomorrow.
b[old] #old : That's what I said, once. Then I got a roof, and a back that aches.
b[carter] #carter : Every town's the same inn with a different name over the door. Take it from me.
b[young] #young : Can I come? I'll carry the bag!
b : And who'd mind the house while you're gone? | Your paws would give out before the Warmwood. | You'd be back in a week, missing your bed.
a@old : Then I'll go before I get the roof.
a@carter : Then I'll judge the inns. Somebody ought to.
a@young : When you're bigger. Then you can carry the both of us.
a : The house can mind itself. | Then I'll limp the rest. | Maybe. But I'd have been.

scene e5_tr_sea_edge
when topic=travel
a : Do you think there's anything past the sea? | Sailors say there's land past the edge of the water. | I'd like to see the sea from a ship, just once. Nothing but water all round.
b[sailor] #sailor : Water, then more water, then more. Then you turn round, if you're lucky.
b[priest] #priest : The Bright One's lands go further than any boat.
b : Sailors say a lot of things. | I'd not get in a boat to find out. | Then go and beg a place on a fishing boat one morning. You'll see.
a@sailor : Then why do you keep going out?
a@priest : I'd still like to see them.
a : Somebody must know. | Nor me, really. | Maybe I will.

scene e5_tr_where_would_you_go
when topic=travel pair.band=friends|spouses|family
a : If you could go anywhere, #pet#, where would it be? | Close your eyes. You're somewhere else. Where? | Tell me the place you'd go, if nobody needed you.
b[from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow] : Up the pass again. Just to breathe that cold, clean air.
b[from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow] : Home to the south. Lie in an orchard and let the apples fall on me.
b[from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater] : Out on Glasswater at dawn with the mist lifting. Nothing in the world better.
b[from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge] : Back to the fens. Don't laugh. I miss the mist.
b[from=oakhaven here.region!=oakhaven] : Under the oaks of the Warmwood. Where it's green and warm and quiet.
b[from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield] : Up on the high downs, with the larks and nothing else.
b[from=ridgemere here.region!=ridgemere] : The wharf at Ridgemere, first thing, with the boats going out.
b : Somewhere I've never been. Anywhere. Just to see it. | Somewhere it never rains. | Nowhere. I'm happy here with you.
a : I'd come with you. | I'll take you, one day. | Well. Good answer.

scene e5_tr_saving_for
when topic=travel a.wealth=poor|comfortable
a : I've been putting a penny a week by for the journey. | Got a little jar for my travelling money. | I'm saving to go and see my sister. Three years now.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : Gracious. And how long will that take you?
b[wealth=poor] #poor : I had a jar like that once. Then the winter came.
b : How much have you got? | Don't tell anyone where it is. | Nearly there?
a@rich : Longer than it'd take you, I expect.
a@poor : This winter it stays in the jar. I've promised myself.
a : Enough for the boots. Not the road. | Under the floor. Don't you tell. | Halfway. Maybe next year.

scene e5_tr_restless_gate
when topic=travel a.drive=restless
cooldown 2d
a : I watched the caravan go out the gate this morning and nearly ran after it. | Every road out of town is calling me and I can't answer any of them. | I'm itching. Everywhere. In my feet mostly.
b[drive=content] #content : Sit down and have some soup. It passes.
b[drive=restless] #restless : I know. #oath#, I know. Let's just go.
b : And what's holding you? | You've always been like this. | Have you tried rolling in something?
a@content : Soup doesn't make it pass. I've tried.
a@restless : Don't tempt me. I'd do it, and you know I would.
a : Everything. Everyone. | Since I was a pup. | Ha. Maybe.

scene e5_tr_too_old
when topic=travel a.stage=old
cooldown 2d
a : I meant to travel. I meant to see the mountains, the sea, the south. | When I was young I'd a map pinned over my bed. | I never did go anywhere. Strange, at the end.
b[young] #young : You could still go! Grandads can travel. And grannies.
b[old] #old : Nor me. We were busy, weren't we? Living.
b : There's still time. | What happened to it? | Did you mind?
a@young : Not with these knees, pup. You go for me.
a@old : We were. And look at us now. Still here.
a : Not with these knees. | The damp got it. And the dreams. | Not really. I had all this instead.

scene e5_tr_wedding_far
when topic=travel pair.band=friends|acquaintances|family
a : My cousin's marrying in another town. I've to walk three days for a dance. | We've a funeral up the road. Two days there and two back for an afternoon. | My aunt wants us all at hers for the feast. All the way out there.
b[carter] #carter : I'm going that way. Sit on the sacks, if you don't mind sacks.
b : That's family. | Take a cart if you can. | Go. You'll be glad you did.
a@carter : I'd sit on a hedgehog to save my paws.
a : That's what I'm afraid of. | Can't afford the cart. | Probably.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The roads and what's on them

scene e5_tr_mud_roads
when topic=travel
a : The roads are all #mud# after the rain. Wheel-deep. | I came in on the road this morning. I'm still finding #mud# in my ears. | A wagon sank to the axle on the road this morning.
b[carter] #carter : Don't. I spent half the day behind my oxen, pushing. They looked at me like it was my fault.
b : The Houses should pave them. | Brush it out before you come in. | They should put stones down.
a@carter : It usually is your fault. Ask the oxen.
a : The Houses don't use them. | Too late. | Who pays for stones?

scene e5_tr_inns
when topic=travel
a : The worst inn I ever stayed at had a goat in the common room. | Best bed I ever had was at a roadside inn. Never found it again. | Do you know any inns on the road worth stopping at?
b[innkeeper] #inn : You'll find none better than mine, and I'll say so to anyone.
b[carter] #carter : I've slept in every inn on every road. The good ones you keep to yourself.
b : Was the goat paying? | Probably a dream. | Sleep in the cart. Cheaper.
a@inn : You would say that.
a@carter : Go on. Just the one. I'll not tell.
a : More than me. | Probably. | And colder.

scene e5_tr_bandits_road
when topic=travel world.bandits=near
a : They say the #bandits# are back on the roads. Nobody's going anywhere. | My cousin's put off coming to visit. Not with them out there. | They say #bandits# stopped a cart and took the very boots off the driver.
b[guard] #guard : We've sent word up the roads. Travel by day and in company till it's dealt with.
b[carter] #carter : I go armed. Not that it's much use against a dozen.
b : Then we all stay home. | Can't blame them. | Barefoot home, poor soul.
a@guard : By day and in company. I'll pass it on.
a@carter : Then don't go. No load's worth that.
a : At least home has a fire. | Not worth the risk. | I'd die of shame.

scene e5_tr_bandits_tale
when topic=travel a.stage=adult|old
cooldown 3d
a : I was robbed on the road once. Years back. | Did I ever tell you about the time I met #bandits#? | I've only seen #bandits# once, and they were the politest thieves you'd ever meet.
b[temper=nervy] : Don't tell me. I'll not sleep tonight. ...Go on, then.
b : What happened? | Go on. | Polite?
a : They took my purse and gave me back my bread and cheese. | They wanted the cart. The horse didn't want to go with them. | Said please and thank you, and took the lot.
b : Generous of them. | Good horse. | Better manners than the toll-keeper.

scene e5_tr_toll
when topic=travel
a : Another toll at the gate. Pay to come in, pay to go out. | They've raised the toll again. | The toll-keeper wanted a penny for my shadow.
b[merchant] #merchant : Tolls are why everything costs what it costs.
b[guard] #guard : Somebody has to pay for the wall.
b : #robbery#. | Of course they have. | And did you pay?
a@merchant : And every penny of it goes on my bill, I notice.
a@guard : The wall was paid for in my grandad's day.
a : I'll climb over it next time. | Next they'll toll the air. | I stood in the shade.

scene e5_tr_walking
when topic=travel
a : I walked in from the next town. My paws are blistered raw. | Walked all day yesterday. My legs are still walking. | Nothing like a long walk to make you love a chair.
b[healer] #healer : Soak your paws in warm salt water tonight, and keep off them tomorrow.
b : You should have taken a cart. | Sit down, then. | True enough.
a@healer : Keep off them? Who's going to do my work?
a : And pay? Never. | If I sit I'll never stand again. | And a fire. And a pie.

scene e5_tr_lost_way
when topic=travel
a : Took a short cut through the woods and got lost for a day. | I followed a footpath on a carter's say-so. It went to a pigsty. | Ever been lost? Properly lost?
b[woodcutter] #wood : Never trust a footpath in a wood. Half of them are made by deer.
b : Short cuts never are. | Carters lie. | Once. In my own town, drunk.
a@wood : Deer! That explains a great deal.
a : I know that now. | Every one of them. | That counts.

scene e5_tr_night_road
when topic=travel here.time=dusk|night
a : Wouldn't want to be on the road tonight. | Someone's still out there, walking in the dark. Poor soul. | You can hear the road at night. Wagons, and other things.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : Don't say that. Shut the shutters.
b[guard] #guard : Nobody honest walks after dark. Bar the watch.
b : Not me. | I hope they've a lantern. | Owls, mostly.
a@nervy : They're shut. I checked twice.
a@guard : Then I'll sleep easy, knowing you're out there.
a : Not anyone with sense. | And a stick. | I'll say owls.

scene e5_tr_journey_weather
when topic=travel here.weather=rain|storm|snow
a : Anyone travelling in this will be soaked to the bone. | Pity anyone on the road today. | The roads will be #mud# for a week after this.
b[carter] #carter : I'm meant to go out tomorrow. I'll be a drowned rat by the gate.
b : Pity the carters. They've no choice. | The inns will be full tonight. | Two weeks.
a@carter : Then stop at the first inn and stay there.
a : Nor the oxen. | Good for the inns. | At least.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Carters and caravans

scene e5_tr_carter_life
when topic=travel a.job=carter
a : Two days out, two days back, and home by Restday if the axle holds. | I've slept under this cart more than in my own bed. | My oxen know the road better than I do. I just hold the reins.
b[carter] #carter : Same here. Mine know which inns give them oats.
b : Doesn't your family mind? | Is it lonely? | Then what are you for?
a@carter : Mine know which ones water the ale, too. They won't stop at those.
a : They say I'm better company in small doses. | The oxen are good listeners. | Singing. Somebody has to sing.

scene e5_tr_caravan_coming
when topic=travel
a : There's a caravan due in. You can hear the bells from the gate. | Look, dust on the road. Caravan coming. | The caravan's late. Two days now.
b[merchant] #merchant : About time. I've an order on that, and folk waiting.
b[young] #young : Can I go and see the oxen? Can I?
b : Good. We need the salt. | Well, somebody's in a hurry. | They're always late. The roads, I expect.
a@merchant : Then you'd best go and meet it.
a@young : Go on, then. Keep out from under the wheels.
a : Everyone does. | Look at them come. | I hope that's all it is.

scene e5_tr_caravan_guard
when topic=travel a.job=guard|soldier
a : I've been asked to ride with a caravan over the downs. Double pay. | I rode with a caravan once. Never been so bored in my life. Then never so frightened. | Caravan guard's an easy job till it isn't.
b : Will you go? | What happened? | Nothing ever happens though, surely.
a : For double pay I'd guard a cabbage. | A night on the downs, and eyes in the gorse. Nothing came. But the eyes. | Once in ten trips. That's when.

scene e5_tr_ride_along
when topic=travel pair.band=acquaintances|friends
a : I've begged a ride on the carter's wagon next week. | A carter said I could sit up front if I paid in pies. | I'm going with the wool wagon. Sitting on the fleeces.
b[carter] #carter : Sit still and don't talk to the oxen. They're working.
b : Hope you've a cushion. | Good pies? | You'll smell like sheep when you get there.
a@carter : I'll talk to the carter, then. All the way.
a : I'll sit on my coat. | My best. | I always smell like sheep.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The other towns, as folk here say
# One who grew up there opens with their own word on it (a[from=...] #own) instead.

scene e5_tr_about_ridgemere
when topic=travel here.region!=ridgemere
a[from=ridgemere] #own : Folk here think everyone in Ridgemere's a lord with a fancy coat. Most of us are wet wolves with sore paws.
a : Been to Ridgemere? All those Houses with their noses in the air. | My cousin went to the city. Came back saying "rather" all the time. | They say it rains every single day in Ridgemere.
b@own : I'd still like to see it. The fancy coats, I mean.
b[from=ridgemere] #rm : We're not all on the Heights, you know. Some of us grew up in the Sump.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : The city has its charms. The Heights are lovely in summer.
b : Pigs on silk cushions, the lot of them. | Ridiculous. | It does. I've been. I was damp for a month.
a@own : You'll see the wet, I promise you.
a@rm : I didn't mean you. Well. Not you especially.
a@rich : So I'm told. I'll never see it.
a : Never been. Never want to. | Rather. | Better them than us.

scene e5_tr_about_frosthollow
when topic=travel here.region!=frosthollow
a[from=frosthollow] #own : They think we're grim, up in Frosthollow. We're not grim. We're cold. It looks the same.
a : Can you imagine living in Frosthollow? Snow half the year. | Frosthollow folk eat their porridge cold, they say, to stay tough. | They say the miners in Frosthollow can see in the dark.
b@own : Grim or cold, I'd not last a winter.
b[from=frosthollow] #fh : We eat it hot like anyone. We've just a stove that never goes out.
b[miner] #miner : Seeing in the dark's just knowing where you put things.
b : I'd freeze in a week. | Mad, the lot of them. | Wouldn't surprise me.
a@own : You'd last. You'd just complain the whole way.
a@fh : Then you'll have to tell me the rest of it, all of it.
a@miner : Still. I'd not go down there.
a : Hard folk, to stick it. | Hard as their rock. | Owls in boots.

scene e5_tr_about_coldwater
when topic=travel here.region!=coldwater
a[from=coldwater] #own : Back in Coldwater they'll be out on the lake about now. I can feel it.
a : Coldwater's the prettiest place in the realm. That lake. | They say Coldwater folk swim in that lake in winter. On purpose. | You can drink straight from the lake in Coldwater, they say.
b@own : You've lake water for blood, you.
b[from=coldwater] #cw : It's true, all of it. And it's colder than you're imagining.
b[fisher] #fisher : Best trout in the realm come out of Glasswater. Don't tell my customers.
b : Too cold for me. | Fools. | Lucky them.
a@own : Cold lake water, aye.
a@cw : I'm imagining very cold.
a@fisher : My lips are sealed. For a trout.
a : Pretty, though. | Brave fools. | Ours tastes of #mud#.

scene e5_tr_about_oakhaven
when topic=travel here.region!=oakhaven
a[from=oakhaven] #own : Folk here say Oakhaven's dark. It's not dark. It's shade. There's a difference.
a : Oakhaven's dark. All those trees. You can't see the sky. | They say in Oakhaven the woods move when you aren't looking. | Oakhaven folk talk to trees. I've seen them do it.
b@own : What's the difference?
b[from=oakhaven] #oak : We do not talk to trees. We talk near trees. Very different.
b[woodcutter] #wood : The Warmwood's honest wood. Best oak in the realm.
b : I'd get lost. | Nonsense. | Somebody has to.
a@own : Shade's friendly. You'd see, if you came.
a@oak : Near them. Of course.
a@wood : Every table in the realm says so.
a : We'd never find you. | So they say. | The trees are probably grateful.

scene e5_tr_about_mirebridge
when topic=travel here.region!=mirebridge
a[from=mirebridge] #own : Say what you like about Mirebridge. Folk here do. Nobody makes a remedy like a fen herbwife.
a : Mirebridge. Wet feet from birth to burial. | They eat eels in Mirebridge. Every day. Every meal. | I went to Mirebridge once. Mist so thick I walked into the chapel.
b@own : I'll grant you the remedies.
b[from=mirebridge] #mb : We eat eels because they're good. You've just never had them done right.
b[healer] #healer : Mirebridge herbs are the best there are. The fens grow everything.
b : Poor souls. | #oath#. | Did you pray while you were in there?
a@own : You'll grant me more than that, one day.
a@mb : I'll take your word. I'll not take the eel.
a@healer : Everything and the damp to go with it.
a : Webbed toes, I shouldn't wonder. | Every one. | I did. For dry feet.

scene e5_tr_about_larkfield
when topic=travel here.region!=larkfield
a[from=larkfield] #own : Folk here think Larkfield's only sheep and haggling. It's the haggling I miss.
a : Larkfield's all sheep. More sheep than wolves. | Larkfield folk would sell you your own coat back. | Every carter in the realm ends up in Larkfield sooner or later.
b@own : I'll not haggle with you, then.
b[from=larkfield] #lf : And you'd thank us for a good price on it.
b[herder] #herder : Larkfield wool's the best in the realm. The downs make it.
b : The sheep probably run it. | Market folk. | Every road meets there.
a@own : Wise.
a@lf : Thank you? I'd count my change.
a@herder : The downs and a good deal of shouting.
a : Better than the Council does. | Sharp as tacks. | And nobody can remember the way out.

scene e5_tr_about_southmeadow
when topic=travel here.region!=southmeadow
a[from=southmeadow] #own : I tell folk here about Southmeadow and they think I'm making it up. The orchards, the sun.
a : Southmeadow's soft. Sun every day and apples falling in your lap. | They say nobody in Southmeadow's ever been cold. | If I had any sense I'd move to Southmeadow.
b@own : You might be. Bring me an apple and I'll believe you.
b[from=southmeadow] #sm : Soft! You try harvest in that heat. Then call me soft.
b[farmer] #farmer : The Sunfields grow grain like nowhere else. But they're dry as a bone.
b : Must be nice. | Lucky things. | Take me with you.
a@own : Next harvest, I'll bring you a sack of them.
a@sm : I'd melt. I'd melt and be glad of it.
a@farmer : Dry and gold. I'd take that over wet and grey.
a : Nice and idle. | Spoilt. | I'll pack.

scene e5_tr_best_town
when topic=travel pair.band=friends|acquaintances
a : Which is the best town, do you think? Honestly. | If you had to live in any other town but this one, which? | Best town in the realm. Go on, name it.
b[from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow] #home : Frosthollow. You'd say different, but you've not seen the snow at dawn.
b[from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow] #home : Southmeadow. Obviously. Have you seen the orchards?
b[from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge] #home : Mirebridge. Nobody bothers you there. Nobody can find you.
b[from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater] #home : Coldwater. You've not lived till you've eaten trout out of Glasswater.
b[from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield] #home : Larkfield. Everything's for sale and everyone's talking.
b : This one, of course. I'm here, aren't I? | Wherever the ale's cheapest. | Wherever my friends are.
a@home : Spoken like someone who misses it.
a : Fair enough. | That's no answer, that's a thirst. | Soft thing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- From elsewhere, standing here (each rests two days)

scene e5_tr_homesick_frosthollow
when topic=travel a.from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow
cooldown 2d
a : It's too warm here. Even in winter. I miss the snow. | Nobody here knows how to light a proper fire. | I miss the sound of the picks in the morning.
b[from=frosthollow] #fh : Me too. We could go back up for midwinter, if the pass is open.
b : Too warm? You're mad. | Show me, then. | That's a noise to miss?
a@fh : We could. We won't, but we could.
a : Probably. | Pine, not oak. And bank it high. | It means everyone's alive.

scene e5_tr_homesick_coldwater
when topic=travel a.from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater
cooldown 2d
a : The water here tastes wrong. Too soft. | I keep looking for the lake when I wake up. | Nobody here knows what a proper trout tastes like.
b[from=coldwater] #cw : I know. I find myself listening for the boats knocking at the jetty.
b[fisher] #fisher : I've got trout. Not lake trout, but trout.
b : Water's water. | Must be strange. | We have fish.
a@cw : Every night. Every single night.
a@fisher : Not lake trout. That's the whole trouble.
a : It is not. | It is. | You have #mud# with fins.

scene e5_tr_homesick_oakhaven
when topic=travel a.from=oakhaven here.region!=oakhaven
cooldown 2d
a : Too much sky here. It makes me dizzy. | I miss the trees. The smell of leaf mould after rain. | Where I come from, the light comes down green.
b[woodcutter] #wood : There's a stand of trees out past the fields. It's not the Warmwood, but it's trees.
b : There's sky everywhere. | It just smells of #mud# here. | Green light? Sounds like a hangover.
a@wood : It's a start. I'll walk out there tonight.
a : Not like this. | Exactly. | It's lovely, you rogue.

scene e5_tr_homesick_mirebridge
when topic=travel a.from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge
cooldown 2d
a : Everything's so dry here. My skin's cracking under my fur. | No mist in the morning. It feels naked. | Nobody here can cook an eel to save their life.
b[from=mirebridge] #mb : I bought a jellied eel off a cart last week. I cried. Not from joy.
b : Most folk like dry. | Most folk like seeing where they're going. | Thank the Bright One.
a@mb : That's what they do to eels up here. It's a crime.
a : Most folk are wrong. | Overrated. | You'd love a proper eel.

scene e5_tr_homesick_larkfield
when topic=travel a.from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield
cooldown 2d
a : No wind here. On the downs the wind never stops. I can't sleep without it. | I miss Marketday back home. The noise of it. | I hear a lark and I'm home again, just for a moment.
b[from=larkfield] #lf : I know. Nobody here even haggles properly. They just pay.
b : Leave a window open. | We have a market. | They're only birds.
a@lf : It's shameful. I've started haggling for them.
a : It's not the same wind. | Not like ours. | Not to me.

scene e5_tr_homesick_southmeadow
when topic=travel a.from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow
cooldown 2d
a : I've not been warm since I came here. Not once. | Your apples are sour. Sorry. They are. | In Southmeadow it's sun on your back from spring to harvest.
b[from=southmeadow] #sm : I know. Nothing up here ripens. Not the apples, not the sun, not the folk.
b : Wear another coat. | Then don't eat them. | And here it's rain on your back.
a@sm : The folk least of all.
a : I've two on. | I can't stop. | Don't I know it.

scene e5_tr_homesick_ridgemere
when topic=travel a.from=ridgemere here.region!=ridgemere
cooldown 2d
a : It's so quiet here at night. No bells, no carts, no shouting. I can't sleep. | I miss the rain. Strange thing to miss. | I miss the city. The wharf, the smell of fish and tar.
b[from=ridgemere] #rm : I miss it too, all of it. You never think you will.
b : You'll get used to it. | We have rain. | Fish and tar? Truly?
a@rm : You don't. Then you do.
a : I hope not. | Not like the city's. | Home's home.

scene e5_tr_what_keeps_you
when topic=travel pair.band=acquaintances|friends
a : What keeps you here, anyway? You could go anywhere. | Ever think of just leaving? Starting over somewhere? | Why do any of us stay put, do you think?
b[married=yes] : Love. Where my other half is, I am.
b[wealth=poor] : Coin. Or the lack of it. You can't eat the road.
b[drive=restless] : Nothing keeps me. I'm off one of these days. You watch.
b[stage=old] : My bones. And my friends' graves. Can't leave them.
b : Habit, mostly. | Every day. Then I have breakfast. | Because it's home. What else?
a : Fair enough. | Ha. Me too. | That's an answer.

scene e5_tr_accent_lost
when topic=travel here.region=ridgemere a.from=ridgemere b.from!=ridgemere b.accent=light
cooldown 3d
a : You've nearly lost your accent, you know. | I can only hear where you're from on certain words. | You sound more like us every year.
b[from=frosthollow] #word : I still say 'deep' for very. Can't help it.
b[from=mirebridge] #word : Only when I'm tired. Then the fens come back.
b[from=coldwater] #word : I still say 'still water' when I meet folk. They look at me oddly.
b[from=oakhaven] #word : I still call the little ones saplings. My neighbours think it's sweet.
b[from=larkfield] #word : I still say 'sold' when I mean yes. Got me into trouble once.
b[from=southmeadow] #word : I still call folk 'my apple'. Mostly by accident.
b : Have I? I hadn't noticed. | Which ones? | That's sad, somehow.
a@word : Keep it. It suits you.
a : I'm sorry. Did you want to keep it? | All the important ones. | You're still you.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Never left

scene e5_tr_never_left
when topic=travel a.stage=old a.accent=broad
cooldown 2d
a : Thirty years and I've not slept a night out of this town. | I've not been past the boundary stone since I was young. Never had to. | Folk ask where I've travelled. I tell them: to the well and back.
b[young] : Never? Not even once? Why?
b[drive=restless] : How? I'd go mad.
b : Proud of it? | And never wanted to? | That's an answer, I suppose.
a : The world comes here. I sit and watch it. | Everything I love is in this town. | Twice a day, every day. Long way, that.

scene e5_tr_never_left_cousin
when topic=travel a.stage=old
cooldown 2d
a : My brother went off to see the world. Came back with a cough and a wife. | My sister went away and never came back. Wrote once. Happy, she said. | Every family loses one to the road.
b[old here.region!=ridgemere] #old : Mine went to the city and came back talking posh.
b : Which did he regret? | Did you believe her? | Some come back.
a@old : Posh! There's no cure for that, they say.
a : He'd not say. | I chose to. | Some.

scene e5_tr_young_away
when topic=travel a.stage=adolescent|young
a : When I'm older I'm going to sign on a ship and sail past the headland! | I'm going to be a carter. See everywhere. | My friend's going away to be apprenticed. In another town!
b[old] #old : Plenty of time for all that. The road'll keep.
b[pair.kin=parent] #parent : Not before your chores, you're not.
b : Good for you. | Mind the bandits. | Lucky friend.
a@old : It might not! It might go somewhere without me.
a@parent : After my chores, then.
a : I will! | I'll fight them. | Not lucky. Lonely, I'll be.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Travellers who pass through

scene e5_tr_travellers_now
when topic=travel here.player_near=yes
a : More travellers through this week than all last season. | Here's another one, look. Dusty boots. | Where do they all come from, these travellers?
b[merchant] #merchant : The road brings coin. I'll not complain.
b[guard] #guard : We keep an eye on them. Most are honest.
b : The road's busy. | Fresh from the road. | And where do they go?
a@merchant : You'll not complain till one leaves without paying.
a@guard : Most.
a : Busy, and noisy. | They always are. | Off somewhere better, I expect.

scene e5_tr_traveller_tale
when topic=travel
a : A traveller told me at the inn there's a lake in the south that's warm as a bath. | Met a traveller who said she'd been to every town in a single month. | A traveller swore he'd seen a wolf with three tails up in the mountains.
b[honesty=guarded] #doubt : Travellers say a lot after a third pint.
b : I'd like to swim in that. | Liar. Or very good boots. | How much had he drunk?
a@doubt : This one was on his fourth.
a : So would I. | Very good boots, I think. | Not enough to explain it.

scene e5_tr_strangers_ask
when topic=travel pair.band=strangers here.player_near=no
a : Are you passing through or stopping? | You've the look of someone who's been on the road. | Long way from home, are you?
b[travelling=yes] #road : Passing through. Off again at first light.
b : Neither. I live here. Three streets over. | Have I? I've only been to the mill and back. | Home's just round the corner.
a@road : Safe roads, then.
a : Oh! Beg pardon. | Must be the boots. | Ah. My mistake.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Pilgrims and holy roads

scene e5_tr_pilgrimage
when topic=travel
a : My mother walked to the Snow Chapel once, on her knees the last mile. | Some folk walk to every chapel in the realm. One a season. | I keep meaning to go on pilgrimage. My knees keep meaning not to.
b[priest] #priest : The road teaches what the chapel can't.
b[piety=devout] #devout : I went up to the Snow Chapel once. The best thing I ever did. And the coldest.
b : On her knees? In the snow? | That's devotion. | Go in summer.
a@priest : Then I'd best get on the road.
a@devout : The coldest. Everyone says that first.
a : Never walked right again. Said it was worth it. | Or boredom. | The Bright One won't mind the season.

scene e5_tr_pilgrim_passing
when topic=travel a.piety=devout
a : Saw pilgrims on the road this morning. Barefoot, singing. | A pilgrim stopped at my door for water. Blessed my house in return. | I've been thinking I should walk to a holy place before I'm too old.
b[piety=devout] #devout : Next time, I'll walk with them. Or with you.
b : Barefoot? On those roads? | Fair trade, water for a blessing. | Before your knees go.
a@devout : We'd keep each other honest.
a : That's the point, I think. | I thought so. | Exactly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The sea

scene e5_tr_sailor_voyage
when topic=travel a.job=sailor
a : We sailed out past the headland and the sea went black as ink. | Saw a whale once, past the Sound. Big as the Rain Hall. | Twelve days out, no land, no wind. Longest days of my life.
b[young] #young : Were you scared? I'd be scared.
b : What's out there? | You're having me on. | What did you do?
a@young : Stiff. Don't tell the crew.
a : Water, and the fear of it. | It looked at me. Then it was gone. | Prayed. Then the wind came.

scene e5_tr_ship_dreams
when topic=travel here.region=ridgemere
a : I watch the ships go out of the Sound and wonder where they end up. | I nearly signed on a ship once. Got as far as the gangplank. | A ship came in from far down the coast with oranges. Oranges!
b[sailor] #sailor : You're not missing much. Wet bunks, salt pork and a bosun who shouts.
b : Somewhere warmer, I hope. | What stopped you? | I've never had one.
a@sailor : And the sea. Don't tell me you don't love the sea.
a : I hope so too. | My mother, on the wharf, crying. | Nor me. I just smelled it.
